{%- comment -%}
  Smooth scrolling — rendered from layout/theme.liquid.

  Lenis is vendored into assets/ rather than pulled from a CDN, per the theme's
  standing rule: a cross-origin request costs DNS, TCP and TLS before a byte
  arrives, and cross-origin cache partitioning means a shared copy is never
  reused across sites anyway.

  Both deferred, and in this order — deferred scripts run in document order, so
  Lenis is defined before bf-scroll.js looks for it.

  Blocking was the first instinct, on the theory that Lenis should own the
  scroll before anyone can scroll. It does not earn it: the worst case is a
  visitor scrolling natively for a few hundred milliseconds before momentum
  takes over, against 18KB of parser-blocking script on every page. The lock
  half is needed only when a drawer opens, which is user-initiated and long
  after load either way.

  bf-scroll.js loads whether or not smooth scrolling is enabled, because it also
  owns the lock the nav drawer and search overlay use. With the setting off it
  falls back to clipping the root element, which is still a better lock than the
  `body { overflow: hidden }` it replaced.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- if settings.smooth_scroll_enabled -%}
  {{ 'lenis.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
  <script src="{{ 'lenis.min.js' | asset_url }}" defer="defer"></script>
{%- endif -%}

<script src="{{ 'bf-scroll.js' | asset_url }}" defer="defer"></script>
